INETD(8)            UNIX Programmer's Manual            INETD(8)



NAME
     inetd - internet super-server

SYNOPSIS
     inetd [-d]

DESCRIPTION
     Inetd listens for connections on behalf of other servers, so a
     service costs nothing until someone uses it.  At startup it
     reads /etc/inetd.conf, binds and listens on the port of each
     service it finds, and then waits in select(2).  When a
     connection arrives it accepts it and starts the configured
     server with the connection as that server's standard input,
     output and error.  The server does not bind anything itself.

     -d      Debug: stay in the foreground, and report each service
             armed, re-armed and handed to a child, and each child
             reaped.

     Each line of /etc/inetd.conf describes one service:

           [host:]service socktype protocol wait server-user \
                   server-program [server-arguments]

     Fields are separated by blanks or tabs.  A line whose first
     character is `#' is a comment, and so is anything after a `#'
     on a line.  The service name is looked up in /etc/services; a
     decimal port number may be given instead.

     Host is the address to bind, as a dotted quad; `*', or no host
     at all, means every address.  Upstream has no host part and
     always binds the wildcard, and so does the shipped file:

           telnet  stream tcp nowait root /testbin/telnetd telnetd -i

     That one line answers the network when an Ethernet card is
     fitted and `telnet localhost' whether or not one is.  A host is
     needed only to serve a single address: 127.0.0.1 keeps a
     service off the network.

     A wildcard listener on a machine with a card holds one of the
     three hardware sockets for as long as inetd runs; a loopback
     listener costs none.  Services past that limit are refused as
     the file is read.

     This inetd honours `stream tcp nowait root' lines.  Every
     other combination is REPORTED and SKIPPED rather than silently
     ignored, so a stock configuration file works with the
     unsupported lines naming themselves in the log.  See BUGS for
     what is missing and why.

     A line asking for an unprivileged server-user is refused, not
     run as root.  Running a service with more privilege than its
     configuration asked for is worse than not running it.

     Inetd detaches with daemon(3), as BSD's does, and reports
     through syslog(3) to syslogd(8).

     A LISTENER IS SINGLE-USE.  On the hardware path accept(2)
     detaches the listener, handing its socket to the accepted
     connection, so inetd rebuilds each listener after every
     connection, reporting `re-armed' under -d.  A service
     whose rebuild fails is carried as down and retried rather than
     dropped, so a transient failure costs one connection and not
     the service.

FILES
     /etc/inetd.conf     the configuration file
     /etc/services       service name to port number

SEE ALSO
     telnetd(8), services(5), telnet(1), syslogd(8)

BUGS
     Only `stream tcp nowait' services run.  Datagram and `wait'
     services are parsed and skipped: the single-threaded model
     hands the socket itself to the server and waits for it, which
     nothing in this system wants yet.

     The built-in services -- echo, discard, chargen, daytime and
     time -- are not provided.  A line naming `internal' is
     skipped.

     There is no privilege dropping, so the server-user field is
     required to read `root'.  Setting it to anything else refuses
     the line.

     At most two services can be armed against the network at once:
     the Ethernet controller has three usable sockets and a live
     connection holds one for its lifetime, so the last is reserved
     rather than handed to a listener.  Configurations past that
     limit are refused as they are read, not partway through.

     A service that cannot arm -- a wire listener with no card
     fitted, say -- is retried on a timer rather than abandoned,
     and each failed attempt is logged.

     TCP wrappers, tcpmux, the RPC services and the connection rate
     limiter are all absent.  A configuration file that relies on
     any of them will be quietly less protected than it looks.



